  "body": "This started as a consistency fix between `Session::EmitClose()` and `Stream::EmitClose()`.\n\nBefore this change, three places decided whether a QUIC application code meant an error, and they did not agree. Session close only considered code `0` clean, so an HTTP/3 peer closing with `H3_NO_ERROR` (`0x100`) could reject `session.closed`. Stream handling recognized `H3_NO_ERROR` in C++, but JavaScript later treated any non-zero reset code as an error. As a result, a clean HTTP/3 reset could still reject `stream.closed` or fail a read.\n\nThe underlying `QuicError` conversion also hardcoded HTTP/3 values without knowing which protocol had been selected. This change moves that decision to code that has that information:\n\n- Raw QUIC keeps `0` as its no-error code.\n- HTTP/3 treats `H3_NO_ERROR` and unknown or reserved codes as no error, as required by RFC 9114.\n- Known HTTP/3 errors, including `H3_DATAGRAM_ERROR`, remain errors.\n\n`QuicError` now preserves the original type and code. Sessions and streams use the selected protocol to interpret them, and JavaScript no longer guesses based on whether the number is zero.\n\nAI assistance: I used Codex's assistance for the investigation/PR",
